#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116fe698-da44-5921-9595-81cea6cf0777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi ({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275632} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb65dd3-22d7-5680-9fd1-2950d61da9b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}(2\\pi -1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275734} {:answer/id #uuid "63ccd069-a545-55f8-89a3-8f411148b91d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276380} {:answer/id #uuid "7d759d00-8c72-5816-95c1-40378ed648d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276620} {:answer/id #uuid "b249fdf3-d35b-5850-8f01-7cb45187798e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi ({e}^{4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b249fdf3-d35b-5850-8f01-7cb45187798e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi ({e}^{4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277073} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd14850f-a094-504b-a27e-fd0a9ea939b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bba8bb12-bb44-5085-86bc-e29c01145558" :question/math-academy-id "q-161053" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ over the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/81/811cfa127ad232e8d5a990998604718a57344d1bd7f43173dc0987631c13db33.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{(r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\pi ({e}^{4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a04819ad-b64f-5339-bd10-f5dae67b0b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276922} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2c3493-79bf-50d5-b228-17395a6342de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277160} {:answer/id #uuid "e51cd9aa-028b-5139-bde8-c1e863ead1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277477} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0a5b30-f93d-53d1-a5dd-3f5cd120beb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277664} {:answer/id #uuid "fb94ab63-cb9a-57db-88b5-198ca54a362e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e51cd9aa-028b-5139-bde8-c1e863ead1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277477} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3194bfd-1758-55d9-8263-07b55c8c710d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f641b0ca-15bb-53b1-96d0-217b8812f929" :question/math-academy-id "q-161054" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ over the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/0a/0a1de805051eff4ea82b4eefb7bb113c724b3d4e7304704831b6294ee4e4d674.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{81}{4}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d7209c-7c17-506b-a4a6-86dde4f2d563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275501} {:answer/id #uuid "22dc0a44-0404-58da-88dd-666cf5f8fdf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275788} {:answer/id #uuid "24f5786b-4836-55d8-ad56-141581c6fdc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275810} {:answer/id #uuid "3edc9dc7-a45f-5d0a-8152-3cecbb7db9af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276022} {:answer/id #uuid "60d9ad63-0c6e-5759-b321-954fef286724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24f5786b-4836-55d8-ad56-141581c6fdc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275810} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3c6d819-dfad-57d8-acd9-b4103349034b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c4a72efd-94bd-57c9-9125-f124ca8a7f81" :question/math-academy-id "q-161055" :question/problem "![](images/71/719f86db69489f62bcbe2753e88cdc6713f04dc6f53415cc2484e2eaab8ffac3.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03227120-1691-54be-9581-ee3778fd0f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275506} {:answer/id #uuid "429cfe66-9dc1-57e0-aefc-d63e0ab79341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276063} {:answer/id #uuid "57adbd5c-f9ae-58db-9b1f-56917ec83b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276261} {:answer/id #uuid "a1990493-12c6-5eb8-8f46-77be04071c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276933} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b0b3be-8afe-561a-847e-c27f91b19430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1990493-12c6-5eb8-8f46-77be04071c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276933} :answer-field/id #uuid "93143ee8-8f7b-58c3-a62c-f096bb59115c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "680e85ad-70a0-5f2a-915e-2d6c3749cdbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161065" :question/problem "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/f0/f0417011f46d3860b7de86fe652a7d80dd9787cb05c2057601b41c17e0c2b423.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/3f/3f4bb1428a6cc9b7e191e1904636d654145d9a1f2fb1dd40843d8cd878194772.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/d6/d6f4e054767650cd2a3d348aee8210af421e2df21792d79f42c5a58b5f4e82e6.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a3a50e4-5411-52b3-a962-4147981c80b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276132} {:answer/id #uuid "971cd55b-3477-5ef3-9184-3dd244f49480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276833} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a719b4-a204-5304-94dc-010ad58340ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276970} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf4833c-b73d-57fe-b477-fadb221b09e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277159} {:answer/id #uuid "cb63beea-6aa8-51c4-97e1-f8b665be72c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5a719b4-a204-5304-94dc-010ad58340ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276970} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3661af2-fdbf-5479-8627-1d09d5073cf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6beb18bc-dc57-5d1c-8540-705d9f63089f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161067" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/86/86036fd86096355b969da695f812c9bf835880edd68f7991183b70a6234e3eb4.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/9e/9ef22dcc15175664331d989bd50d9a8d60a354a0780dbaf9465caca04f28c2d9.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/f7/f724daf8321195815c9e413aab2106c611bc9d2e9653939266fc828aebeca68c.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b90531f-8c48-528f-be8c-e6dbd51abb0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275998} {:answer/id #uuid "52d5e922-bccb-5fce-b300-691e090faea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276212} {:answer/id #uuid "7df546c6-3376-55f8-94c7-e5be74bd7136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276624} {:answer/id #uuid "acefb97e-6196-52ba-a16d-9390374236cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277032} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3e9490-2677-5e30-aba6-03b5cf96c58e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b90531f-8c48-528f-be8c-e6dbd51abb0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275998} :answer-field/id #uuid "5007391b-6a4b-5cf3-b468-d5880451e5ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4206eea6-b522-5853-935f-b0d344e48a0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161070" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,\\,y\\le 0\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/d8/d882bf70ecb7b94eee0eeeffbf63874ca8607268167f96278deb4678ec3b827a.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(4,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/60/605d9ce1c9030d1ac83c0a320bd648b42893f222279dd2d56b111acd9957c202.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/f3/f37e34042338af2b1ba5f0c214ebf046eeba068b0b3238b662910a868c069ee4.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1395d175-7d1f-53b0-a292-a6d896cb7078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}({r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275659} {:answer/id #uuid "34ba6316-25bc-5c60-8cd0-fefb9a09fcd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275949} {:answer/id #uuid "43e22192-319a-513d-a28f-5db2ed854a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\left(\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276077} {:answer/id #uuid "7b94d82f-c6ee-525c-b64f-d572bc15262c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276600} {:answer/id #uuid "e1dc77eb-167e-5632-829d-ad58f36473c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b94d82f-c6ee-525c-b64f-d572bc15262c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276600} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa5eff7c-3120-504b-a38a-efaff47fe6cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d548092f-8e97-501c-97b5-1d5ab43c5b4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161079" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4da25a7fcce468774aafcf2c1dbf7b4ef65089194d0330b1159d18a9a8f4bfe9.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the plane is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{ }dA & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ac000c-1758-5e85-9be7-c02c06f56199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275539} {:answer/id #uuid "167a7194-5754-55f4-8e86-e2579f03ffce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275686} {:answer/id #uuid "365bc493-05f6-5b56-921d-bd416a928820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275962} {:answer/id #uuid "9b303eed-2f56-5daf-b733-6f11cc63c4e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276869} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6cc47b-0553-5b4a-8ebe-c0cfd59c1221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06ac000c-1758-5e85-9be7-c02c06f56199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275539} :answer-field/id #uuid "0852c563-c2ff-5b18-be16-1d7ac03b0110" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f11e52b-1477-5101-9ca1-e393f690fd0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161101" :question/problem "![](images/ae/aed70cc2e5495fcfd9f42ff5d79c3371fb17b62e12ab1909c7738071e59f1a23.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=x+2,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(-1,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2 & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ r(r-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).$\n\n- For $y=x+2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/b9/b9c2cd48302ef8d4e2d5e65a56d19506600c5e6552a4d9a1ef7d578a09a21557.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\le r\\le 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10db7571-c250-563b-b925-4a7669bb0915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275627} {:answer/id #uuid "191c25d1-e662-5470-8eea-07f7ede108e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275710} {:answer/id #uuid "26e4c4a7-016c-5aa7-b9f0-9b2d2b8e2ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275831} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a0fd28-3760-55bd-b4cd-551d81da6733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277003} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9caf25-20ec-56ed-b184-ae2940d69e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10db7571-c250-563b-b925-4a7669bb0915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275627} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8e19673-91fc-5bda-8e2c-95da05a9da2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e1a8f74-9fd7-53da-9313-229356fec0da" :question/math-academy-id "q-161114" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b5b5e82eb8dca9ae71c08b306ac4618500c7c3af4aa0858297d5585e055007f.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $4$ and $6,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $4$ and $6$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36,$ we obtain $r=6.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,4\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{6}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{4}^{6}\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {[r]}_{4}^{6}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (6-4)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "293b93fd-889c-517c-9d3a-26399a5703e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275850} {:answer/id #uuid "536e21e0-3026-52b1-a74a-837f8ea761fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276218} {:answer/id #uuid "d6855a66-a3f1-50f7-99a2-ef4f510b168a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277367} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9e5c99-3d31-585d-9494-757b8cd39675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277418} {:answer/id #uuid "e086ee12-fbd8-5958-ba36-3bac2e4871dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd9e5c99-3d31-585d-9494-757b8cd39675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277418} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5f8f5db-59a7-5898-a633-ab39ca74a59b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06fc34bf-ed22-543d-8e30-2936b87d0b0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161115" :question/problem "![](images/63/636c9e587d6cbb9fcf9b24d068520330d5365cf80a2800bdd8244698512be3c0.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $5,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $1$ and $5$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ we obtain $r=5.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{3/2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{r}^{3}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[-\\frac{1}{r}]}_{1}^{5}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e874ac0-e65b-5360-a3ae-8d3cee9a293c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{136}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276174} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6d0727-a8c7-54c1-a052-0ab674c285ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276480} {:answer/id #uuid "9a24e4a4-5fae-53df-b0ac-a94ecd44080a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{272}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276858} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e75f34-62d5-55ac-88c2-d837f8b52349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276942} {:answer/id #uuid "c8552123-1f18-5426-ae62-d6a0a73fe4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "272" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a24e4a4-5fae-53df-b0ac-a94ecd44080a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{272}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276858} :answer-field/id #uuid "83592cd5-dcfa-53a6-957a-c55bf8f75c62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60159fed-8119-56a1-9a58-db906033ca8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161121" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cd21fbe460f530f08bc8e85f407f9d6ac49b263d3a5361af093a21cd1ca8d11.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is a half annulus enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $5,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the half-annulus between the disks of radii $3$ and $5$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ we obtain $r=5.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/da/da18aafec278b5d44b0530097729e4cb4b319e219c005fc155d78fab8aa90937.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta <\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{5}{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{625}{4}-\\frac{81}{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =136{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =136{[-\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =136\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{272}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10279bac-75c2-5820-bf33-84359cbf720e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7u+4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275617} {:answer/id #uuid "397e53f3-63a0-518a-999e-8c3d8631bb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4u+7v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275987} {:answer/id #uuid "897e0260-ef83-5fe5-9ee2-95ed9d4cd1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7u+8v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276718} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3f2f9b-ef08-5972-b94d-02717ccd84df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8u-7v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277162} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6d4a87-66ae-58b1-806a-631ade59590d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7u-8v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10279bac-75c2-5820-bf33-84359cbf720e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7u+4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275617} :answer-field/id #uuid "8692e3d1-c9bd-5cca-9274-6af9e13d6963" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0bf481ef-95d3-5b3f-b324-29ecd57febda" :question/math-academy-id "q-161358" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=3x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 5$ and $-3\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,3u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 5,\\,-3\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=3u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨3u-2v,\\,-v,\\,2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3u-2v,\\,-v,\\,2u⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(3u-2v)\\cdot (-3)+(-v)\\cdot (2)+2u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-9u+6v-2v+2u \\\\  & =-7u+4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-7u+4v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(-7u+4v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1522d126-bc34-5af8-9b6a-e28d9a69cc28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275676} {:answer/id #uuid "18a12e63-447a-54f3-aa1b-7655d054b2a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275705} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0d9dd7-0a4c-53a0-93f3-266c540e5806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276630} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ec1fd9-ea87-51b3-9aac-bec86160b60b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277443} {:answer/id #uuid "eb954333-7f24-5dd3-958d-038c55f1b656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb954333-7f24-5dd3-958d-038c55f1b656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277536} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7b5f25c-b535-59ae-8da4-9392f8bb667d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "154ea74b-7c8a-5b64-880d-29244a657638" :question/math-academy-id "q-161382" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xyz⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xyz⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\  & =⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xy(2x+y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-x}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{-y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{xy(2x+y)}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xyz⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-x)\\cdot 2-(-y)\\cdot (1)+xy(2x+y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+y+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+\\frac{1}{2}+{x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{7x}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{x}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11f73d79-8055-5bca-ad98-afa9fe1eca21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{e}^{2}-8e}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275639} {:answer/id #uuid "2489ad8d-d47c-5d5d-acf8-3774325d7221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{e}^{2}-8e-1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275804} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4a22ca-44c7-5916-87eb-4e511e7bfbd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8e-1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275885} {:answer/id #uuid "e441be55-99f3-5307-823b-61fbeb9c2f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{e}^{2}-1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277470} {:answer/id #uuid "edd11e5f-f65e-5f0e-a5f8-5cb3f2bfbc56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{e}^{2}+1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2489ad8d-d47c-5d5d-acf8-3774325d7221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{e}^{2}-8e-1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275804} :answer-field/id #uuid "b80fe3bf-3f3b-576e-826d-db2fe76a6dda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5fb8cb6-33d1-582b-8cf3-53ceebac82e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161435" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{z}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{z}⟩{|}_{z=x+y} \\\\  & =⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{x+y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{xy}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{x+y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{{e}^{x+y}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{z}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(xy)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot 1+{e}^{x+y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-xy-x-y+{e}^{x+y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-xy-x-y+{e}^{x+y})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}-xy-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{x+y}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{2x}-{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{2x}-{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}-{e}^{x}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-e)-(0+\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2}-8e-1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b5412c-a9ab-5d1e-8d26-9a4c8f27c8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2u+7v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275625} {:answer/id #uuid "16ffd34a-40d1-5df6-87ca-5595406b5509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4u+v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275691} {:answer/id #uuid "85617ab2-8072-5a8d-9456-259cf0bef230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4v-u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276672} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5a4f92-85cc-5237-ba98-a4e395360811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2u+v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277305} {:answer/id #uuid "fe69b9d6-2d90-53f7-90a8-f4819b62096e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u+4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10b5412c-a9ab-5d1e-8d26-9a4c8f27c8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2u+7v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275625} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a3b6104-863d-512e-9bea-c3b626c9b6e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "279f5fbf-fc31-5667-8b25-7d1c25dc2d02" :question/math-academy-id "q-161441" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y-z,\\,x+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x,\\,y-z,\\,x+y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,v-(u-2v),\\,u+v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,3v-u,\\,u+v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u,\\,3v-u,\\,u+v⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-1)+(3v-u)\\cdot 2+(u+v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-u+6v-2u+u+v \\\\  & =-2u+7v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\,y-z,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2u+7v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2u+7v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7082e8bb-b180-5b74-b8a7-e85890274ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-Q$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276491} {:answer/id #uuid "90563b64-bf19-5230-a35a-721a99920cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-Q$ and $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276784} {:answer/id #uuid "91780ef2-9a71-57a1-99ea-245bc6f522d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276795} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8f03ec-bd00-5c8c-b9b2-ca5f6b5f96f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277155} {:answer/id #uuid "ed73198a-0a2c-58e6-bfa9-608aaaadffc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-R$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7082e8bb-b180-5b74-b8a7-e85890274ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-Q$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276491} :answer-field/id #uuid "2729d011-05e6-5f9a-aa2c-45fe935285a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1aa60ad7-eedc-5a6c-9adc-f826e8cc15e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161446" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $x=k(y,z)$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(P+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\frac{∂k}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=k(y,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(y,z)=⟨k(y,z),\\,y,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂z},\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂y} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂z} & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P+(-Q\\,)\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\frac{∂k}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0643aa87-cd34-546b-808f-baf7069ddd3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2P$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275535} {:answer/id #uuid "33a51edf-b815-559f-bf1a-c079952737b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $-2R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275936} {:answer/id #uuid "363514bb-3d3f-5b6d-8842-2ca7a25acfe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275960} {:answer/id #uuid "608d35ab-75b6-598e-b2f0-82ca279404a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $2R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276340} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6360fb-9cce-5dca-8b8d-0788f42c0291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2P$ and $-2R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0643aa87-cd34-546b-808f-baf7069ddd3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2P$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275535} :answer-field/id #uuid "3292da62-41fc-5075-93e6-a9828fa8aabd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ce5730b3-fc65-5b4c-bdd5-95a34bf939c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161453" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $y=-{x}^{2}z$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,xz+Q+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $j$-component.*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=-{x}^{2}z,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,z)=⟨x,\\,-{x}^{2}z,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,-2xz,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,-{x}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2xz & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{x}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-2xz,\\,-1,\\,-{x}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′}=-{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′}=⟨2xz,\\,1,\\,{x}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,R\\,⟩\\cdot ⟨2xz,\\,1,\\,{x}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2P\\,xz+Q+R\\,{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1030e7c9-e96e-503f-967b-785d007daa76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2Q$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275620} {:answer/id #uuid "144499aa-4e39-58cc-9e9a-8a50dd3383d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2Q$ and $-2R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275665} {:answer/id #uuid "68ab0b1a-abea-51fe-b67c-1548bc01d191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276417} {:answer/id #uuid "a76e424f-dd86-5864-94a5-584b2a311108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $-2R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276987} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b4e30b-9426-50d8-bc2f-4878ecf2feae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-Q$ and $-R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "144499aa-4e39-58cc-9e9a-8a50dd3383d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2Q$ and $-2R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275665} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e4956ee-4770-5be1-83ec-b19c8e91c7c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6603224-9e78-54fd-8272-af20dda792ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-161456" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $x=2yz$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(P+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,z+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=2yz,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(y,z)=⟨2yz,\\,y,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨2z,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨2y,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2z & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2y & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨1,\\,-2z,\\,-2y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(y,z))\\cdot ({r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-2z,\\,-2y⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P+(\\,-2Q)\\,z+(\\,-2R)\\,y\\,)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002ddc9f-2c71-5478-8c1d-77a6a8c334bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3u-3v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275480} {:answer/id #uuid "023841e2-2821-56a0-955a-89084c15f8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u+v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275496} {:answer/id #uuid "4d835b30-d2dd-53ba-9310-a5a2961e3b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u+2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276160} {:answer/id #uuid "b4df5cbd-8643-5942-bb9e-8d044eb77815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u-3v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277092} {:answer/id #uuid "f42daa7a-2c08-5500-aa68-d0405d0dc904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3v+u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "002ddc9f-2c71-5478-8c1d-77a6a8c334bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3u-3v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275480} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca208624-88d6-5a85-8572-2dd062347b32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06f1e075-9f39-52f0-b9b8-c179d2533ffc" :question/math-academy-id "q-161462" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y+2z,\\,2x-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=y-x$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,v-u⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,y+2z,\\,2x-y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=v-u} \\\\  & =⟨v-u,\\,v+2(v-u),\\,2u-v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v-u,\\,3v-2u,\\,2u-v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨v-u,\\,3v-2u,\\,2u-v⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(v-u)\\cdot 1+(3v-2u)\\cdot (-1)+(2u-v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =v-u-3v+2u+2u-v \\\\  & =3u-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,y+2z,\\,2x-y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3u-3v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(3u-3v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e71243-92a0-5d43-b5a3-d37e9d9577d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-Q,\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂z},\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275638} {:answer/id #uuid "1733f81c-8c75-5995-ad54-2ab255bb13b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-Q,\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂x},\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275694} {:answer/id #uuid "2cfc931d-121f-57c0-ac5c-fdc2916d3343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-Q,\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275883} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8033d3-73a6-5faf-9ba8-27ae7a3acd02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q,\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275991} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2411ed-300b-54b9-9625-f35ce1fd17dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q,\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f2411ed-300b-54b9-9625-f35ce1fd17dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q,\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276182} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fceae9a-bc1a-5dd0-a3ba-10a17494da0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46dbd1e0-5399-5e33-b950-1f08b8d717ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-161465" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $x=k(y,z)$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(P-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}-R\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=k(y,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(y,z)=⟨k(y,z),\\,y,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂z},\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂y} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂z} & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P-Q\\,\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\,\\frac{∂k}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f8ea45-7189-5de7-b023-513085bc87a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275684} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8d5990-f27c-5d2a-8556-ade0d064ae64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275747} {:answer/id #uuid "9b81db63-fffe-50ae-abb4-769c30bdcdcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276871} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7a2355-821a-5abf-ae21-cfb859e97e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277145} {:answer/id #uuid "e9752611-528c-5fdc-8927-863cf9474421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d8d5990-f27c-5d2a-8556-ade0d064ae64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275747} :answer-field/id #uuid "6db629ff-d6ea-571f-9225-53d34e230d79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da0b12bf-561c-5907-8348-62e84bca5b63" :question/math-academy-id "q-161481" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ for $-\\pi \\le x\\le 0$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{1}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))-0\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+0)-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{-\\pi }^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-0+(-\\pi )-\\frac{(-\\pi )^{3}}{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b71688e-a084-50b0-8ee6-cfbf6ebd32af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e-{e}^{-1}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275872} {:answer/id #uuid "4431028f-ab75-5b42-a52f-32fd75e489e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-1}-e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276079} {:answer/id #uuid "745d7976-0dbc-50a2-9490-bc45b40b9a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5e+2{e}^{-1}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276530} {:answer/id #uuid "89b5e633-6fda-572c-a63c-6a89752bac4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7e-{e}^{-1}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276721} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b62c25-f5f9-5ac7-a005-6cbd37efb361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3e+{e}^{-1}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4431028f-ab75-5b42-a52f-32fd75e489e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-1}-e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276079} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b538e17-dfb5-54b8-b49d-76ddb3c1edcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d858f67e-e87d-5922-848f-1cf10db83bc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161536" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩{|}_{z={e}^{x}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{x+{e}^{x}}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot ({e}^{x})-(x+{e}^{x})\\cdot 0+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-y{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[-\\frac{{y}^{2}{e}^{x}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{[{e}^{x}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}-e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55b0b9b9-6c61-52a0-883d-72dabb5dc5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276237} {:answer/id #uuid "70f28e28-c030-5053-a069-7bc3877eb33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276493} {:answer/id #uuid "80ad5cf6-f9ff-56a1-bcb6-20cadcc92134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276642} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8eb565-31a6-5155-8e44-4a4d6a7de0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277276} {:answer/id #uuid "fce9c2cc-cb68-5fd2-aff3-f988333f015d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55b0b9b9-6c61-52a0-883d-72dabb5dc5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276237} :answer-field/id #uuid "5439730e-b587-501e-b283-51c217f9d9d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d38df384-c529-5d23-b152-2f8b3a09208a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161539" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy))=\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy))=\\frac{x}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy)} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{1}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({cos}^{2}⁡(xy))\\cdot \\left(\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}\\right)-({cos}^{2}⁡(xy))\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}\\right)+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-x+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(-y-x+1)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-xy+y]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-{x}^{2}+x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ee01a9e-4d6e-5878-adf2-060b8c43af0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7(3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276023} {:answer/id #uuid "868095d1-9b6f-50d0-a2e4-14ea341f041e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276684} {:answer/id #uuid "be04cd4e-9167-5e17-85e8-8eda77bf9d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7(3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277171} {:answer/id #uuid "ea250f8f-2bf8-51ef-a17f-c4811c95105a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7(6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277523} {:answer/id #uuid "f818f75a-e762-544b-8f16-1c1ee72458c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea250f8f-2bf8-51ef-a17f-c4811c95105a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7(6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277523} :answer-field/id #uuid "5926af28-c362-5c51-8ef9-27b5b281cdda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1788e522-3caf-5313-8725-17c2fd98bec0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161541" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2\\sqrt{xy}$ for $3\\le x\\le 4$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{xy})=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{xy})=\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩{|}_{z=2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{4xy+1}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}+4xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{[-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x{y}^{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+8x+0-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16be5565-2c3b-5f69-b524-980e1e2bf3d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275687} {:answer/id #uuid "17111300-baf7-5ae1-b074-f80c3453c930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275692} {:answer/id #uuid "32cd6ba9-9960-5d19-8ac0-24c02b4d1256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275928} {:answer/id #uuid "d089aab2-9fb8-5519-be05-cd9b1526a968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277322} {:answer/id #uuid "da5ba4ac-a3ff-57ea-8b84-e611e12b0b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da5ba4ac-a3ff-57ea-8b84-e611e12b0b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277393} :answer-field/id #uuid "e911db97-27c7-54f3-ad45-0562d2a959a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "caf172ee-9d85-5048-a736-0f63395a41ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-161542" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩{|}_{z=1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x+y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{y-x}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+y)\\cdot (2x)-(y-x)\\cdot (2y)+1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2{x}^{2}-2xy-2{y}^{2}+2xy+1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[y-{x}^{2}y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=-1}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[(1-{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3})-(-1+{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3})]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2x-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(2-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3})-(-2+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a2c4b7e-5a81-5de2-94d6-7167bf955b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276431} {:answer/id #uuid "858fcbe8-2cf5-5ca9-990f-91adfe85964d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276674} {:answer/id #uuid "ab2313fb-5b8b-58b3-a0a5-5f823fb6971d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277015} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f28b55-e6f6-5262-ba0f-bd065f0baf55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277069} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ac136e-0642-5dac-b405-2d869dca7fa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a2c4b7e-5a81-5de2-94d6-7167bf955b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276431} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7fe5238-ca86-56aa-9732-ac63987785d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0720c99-cc1f-5800-96bf-793fcc7002e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161552" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2x(y+z)⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2x(y+z)⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2xz⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\ F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2y(2x+y),\\,2x(y+2x+y)⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{3{x}^{3}y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{4xy+2{y}^{2}}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{4xy+4{x}^{2}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2x(y+z)⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(3{x}^{3}y)\\cdot (2)-(4xy+2{y}^{2})\\cdot (1)+4xy+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6{x}^{3}y-2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6{x}^{3}y-2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[-3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}+4{x}^{2}y]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{x}^{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{2x}{3}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-12-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ab9136-37aa-5486-8d72-1df4b92b5e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/89/89f1923268dfb0d128f508439a5c14ba296d20adc609421a5c6c2f767e6862e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278742} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca40a80-5d61-51f7-8e3e-75d26e54510d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7c/7c4b8ca9a1a6083022f5e9e8eaddfae25b9d1541313b5e19e0f71d0d94214003.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278856} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7b1a7c-e6f8-5a6b-956f-01d3b6f24543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2a/2a60ced10ea66c155a4cdc2190e8dc007e01f3724f4afb047550d39909000c34.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279278} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7735e7-1f44-5771-a1e3-9e9212adab26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b0/b0356c622c6656688e54f3ab6e3bf86dd36b6007579dca04e7553a4e2b22693e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280241} {:answer/id #uuid "f207af7f-2793-5d2a-91b0-b63e8b00a0f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/84/84c5e6aaa9dfc0312f2a15ed24ce3326f0c811513d3fac22d558bf233fd1b5dc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c7b1a7c-e6f8-5a6b-956f-01d3b6f24543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2a/2a60ced10ea66c155a4cdc2190e8dc007e01f3724f4afb047550d